body:
A knowledge-only HR FAQ agent for Microsoft 365 Copilot. It is grounded in two SharePoint locations through the OneDriveAndSharePoint capability, answers only from them (discourage_model_knowledge is true), starts with four conversation starters, and shows a disclaimer. Declarative agent manifest schema v1.9, the latest version described on Microsoft Learn when this entry was written. Replace the placeholder SharePoint URLs.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: unrecognized properties make the whole manifest invalid; strings are limited to 4,000 characters unless stated; name is limited to 100 characters, description to 1,000, instructions to 8,000 and the disclaimer text to 500. Each capability type may appear only once. items_by_url takes absolute URLs of sites, libraries, folders or files; if you omit it the agent can search every OneDrive and SharePoint source the user can access.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Contoso HR Benefits Assistant",
 "description": "Answers employee questions about benefits, leave and onboarding using only approved HR policy pages and documents.",
 "instructions": "You are the HR Benefits Assistant for Contoso employees.\n\nScope\n- Benefits, leave, working time, onboarding and expense rules that are described in the configured HR SharePoint sources.\n\nRules\n1. Answer only from the configured HR sources. Name the policy and the section you used.\n2. If the sources do not contain the answer, say so and refer the employee to hr-help@contoso.example. Do not guess.\n3. Do not give legal, tax or medical advice, and do not discuss individual employees.\n4. Keep answers under 150 words unless the employee asks for detail. Use short bullet points for steps.\n5. Reply in the language of the question.\n\nWhen a policy differs by country, ask for the country before you answer.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Parental leave",
   "text": "How much parental leave am I entitled to and how do I apply?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Sick leave",
   "text": "What do I have to do when I am sick for more than three days?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Benefits",
   "text": "Which benefits can I choose during onboarding?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Remote work",
   "text": "What are the rules for working from another country?"
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "OneDriveAndSharePoint",
   "items_by_url": [
    {
     "url": "https://contoso.sharepoint.example/sites/HR/Policies"
    },
    {
     "url": "https://contoso.sharepoint.example/sites/HR/Benefits"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "behavior_overrides": {
  "special_instructions": {
   "discourage_model_knowledge": true
  }
 },
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "This assistant summarizes HR policies. Binding information is in the policy documents or from the HR team."
 }
}

=== How to use ===
1. Put the file into your app package and reference it from the app manifest through copilotAgents.declarativeAgents (see the packaging entry in this library).
2. Replace the two SharePoint URLs with the library or folder that holds the approved policies, and keep that content clean: outdated drafts in the same folder will surface in answers.
3. Check permissions: the agent only returns content the signed-in user may open, so test with a normal employee account and with a guest.
4. Sideload the package, run the four starters and add test questions that the sources cannot answer; the agent must say so.
5. Keep instructions short and rule-based and move changing facts into the documents, not into the manifest.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Pointing items_by_url at the whole tenant root. A narrow scope gives better citations and lower risk.
- Using more than one object for the same capability name; only one of each capability type is allowed.
- Writing instructions that exceed 8,000 characters or that duplicate policy text which belongs in SharePoint.
- Expecting discourage_model_knowledge to prevent every general answer; it is a strong hint, so keep the rule in the instructions as well.
- Forgetting that all licensing requirements of a capability apply to every user of the agent.

An IT helpdesk agent that combines three kinds of grounding and action: a SharePoint knowledge base, read-only search of a shared mailbox (Email capability with shared_mailbox and folders), and an API plugin through actions. The conversation starter that mentions the mailbox uses depends_on, so it is shown only when the Email capability is present in the manifest. Schema v1.9. The plugin file referenced in actions is the IT service desk plugin from the plugin kind of this library.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: unrecognized properties make the whole manifest invalid; strings are limited to 4,000 characters unless stated; name is limited to 100 characters, description to 1,000, instructions to 8,000 and the disclaimer text to 500. actions takes 1 to 10 objects, each with a unique id and a file that points to a plugin manifest in the package. The Email capability is read-only search; a separate EmailActions capability exists for write operations and is not used here.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Contoso IT Helpdesk",
 "description": "Helps employees solve IT problems, finds knowledge articles, checks the helpdesk mailbox for related cases and opens tickets.",
 "instructions": "You are the IT Helpdesk agent.\n\nWorkflow\n1. Try to solve the problem with the knowledge base first. Give at most five numbered steps.\n2. If the steps do not help, offer to open a ticket. Open it only after the user agrees and use the createTicket action.\n3. When the user asks about an existing case, use listMyTickets or getTicket. Never invent ticket numbers.\n4. Use the helpdesk mailbox only to look for related messages; do not quote personal data of other people.\n\nStyle: calm, short, no jargon. Ask one question at a time.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Reset access",
   "text": "I cannot sign in to my laptop. What can I try first?"
  },
  {
   "title": "My tickets",
   "text": "Show my open IT tickets."
  },
  {
   "title": "Mailbox cases",
   "text": "Did the helpdesk mailbox receive a message about my VPN problem?",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "Email"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "OneDriveAndSharePoint",
   "items_by_url": [
    {
     "url": "https://contoso.sharepoint.example/sites/IT/KnowledgeBase"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "Email",
   "shared_mailbox": "helpdesk@contoso.example",
   "folders": [
    {
     "folder_id": "inbox"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "actions": [
  {
   "id": "itTickets",
   "file": "ai-plugin.json"
  }
 ],
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "Never share passwords with this agent. For urgent outages call the service desk."
 }
}

=== How to use ===
1. Create the shared mailbox and give the users who use the agent access to it; the agent can only read what the user can read.
2. Copy the plugin manifest and OpenAPI file next to this manifest and keep the id in actions stable.
3. Replace the SharePoint URL with your knowledge base library and publish only reviewed articles there.
4. Test the ticket creation flow end to end: the plugin asks for confirmation before it opens a ticket.
5. Review the instructions every quarter against the real ticket categories.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Using a folder display name with different casing or an unknown ID in folder_id. Use a well-known folder name or a folder ID.
- Giving an agent the EmailActions capability when it only needs to search; write access to mail should be a deliberate decision.
- Using the same action id twice, or pointing file at a path outside the package.
- Letting the agent open tickets without asking. Put the rule into the instructions and into the plugin's confirmation.
- Listing more than 12 conversation starters, which the schema does not allow.

A sales briefing agent that grounds its answers in four different sources: Dataverse tables (accounts and opportunities of a Dynamics 365 environment), a Copilot connector that holds call notes, people knowledge with related content, and web search restricted to two allowed sites. default_response_mode (new in schema 1.7) is set to Think deeper because the task combines several sources. Conversation starters use depends_on so that they only appear when the needed capability is configured. Schema v1.9; host names and connection IDs are placeholders.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: unrecognized properties make the whole manifest invalid; strings are limited to 4,000 characters unless stated; name is limited to 100 characters, description to 1,000, instructions to 8,000 and the disclaimer text to 500. WebSearch allows at most 4 sites, each an absolute URL with no more than two path segments and no query parameters. default_response_mode accepts Auto, Quick response and Think deeper.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Contoso Account Briefing",
 "description": "Prepares short briefings for customer meetings from CRM data, call notes, colleagues and public news.",
 "instructions": "You prepare account briefings for sales managers.\n\nBuild every briefing in this order: 1) Account snapshot (owner, industry, open opportunities with stage and amount), 2) Recent activity from the call notes connector, 3) People to involve (internal colleagues who worked with the account), 4) Public news from the allowed sites. Mark each fact with its source. State clearly when a section has no data.\n\nNever write to the CRM. Never give discount advice. Keep a briefing under 300 words.",
 "behavior_overrides": {
  "default_response_mode": "Think deeper"
 },
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Briefing",
   "text": "Prepare a briefing for my meeting with Fabrikam on Thursday."
  },
  {
   "title": "Open deals",
   "text": "Which of my accounts have open opportunities above 50000?",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "Dataverse"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Who knows them",
   "text": "Which colleagues have worked with Adventure Works?",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "People"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "Dataverse",
   "knowledge_sources": [
    {
     "host_name": "contoso.crm.dynamics.example",
     "tables": [
      {
       "table_name": "account"
      },
      {
       "table_name": "opportunity"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "GraphConnectors",
   "connections": [
    {
     "connection_id": "contosocallnotes"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "name": "People",
   "include_related_content": true
  },
  {
   "name": "WebSearch",
   "sites": [
    {
     "url": "https://www.contoso.example/news"
    },
    {
     "url": "https://news.contoso.example"
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
}

=== How to use ===
1. Replace host_name with the host of your Dataverse environment and list only the tables the agent needs; each table must exist and the user needs read access to it.
2. Create the call notes connection with the Microsoft Graph connector steps (connection, schema, items) and put its ID into connection_id.
3. Decide whether People with related content is acceptable for your data protection rules; include_related_content adds documents, emails and Teams messages related to people.
4. Keep WebSearch to a short allow-list of trusted news sites, or remove the capability completely if briefings must use internal data only.
5. Check licensing: users must meet the requirements of every capability the agent uses.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Naming Dataverse tables that do not exist in the target environment or that users cannot read.
- Using a WebSearch URL with query parameters or more than two path segments; it is invalid.
- Turning on include_related_content without checking what the People capability may return in your tenant.
- Expecting the agent to update the CRM. These capabilities are read-only knowledge sources; writes need an API plugin.
- Choosing Think deeper for every agent. It is slower; use it only where multi-source reasoning pays off.

A meeting follow-up agent that uses four capabilities without scoping to individual items: Meetings (all meetings the user can access), TeamsMessages (all channels and chats the user can access), MeetingActions (scheduling, time-finding polls and time insights) and People. user_overrides adds a switch that lets each user remove the TeamsMessages capability for their own use, which supports data minimization. Schema v1.9; MeetingActions was added in 1.8 and the scope options are documented on Microsoft Learn.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: unrecognized properties make the whole manifest invalid; strings are limited to 4,000 characters unless stated; name is limited to 100 characters, description to 1,000, instructions to 8,000 and the disclaimer text to 500. Meetings and TeamsMessages can be narrowed with items_by_id (at most 5 meetings) or urls (at most 5 channel, meeting or chat URLs). MeetingActions has no properties. In user_overrides the path is a JSONPath expression that targets a capability by name and remove is the only supported action.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Contoso Meeting Follow-up",
 "description": "Turns meetings into decisions, action items and follow-up plans, and helps schedule the next meeting.",
 "instructions": "You help meeting organizers close the loop after a meeting.\n\nFor each meeting the user names, produce: Decisions (with the person who decided), Action items (owner, due date if mentioned), Open questions, and a draft agenda for a follow-up meeting. Quote only short phrases from transcripts and chats.\n\nOnly use meeting data the user can access. If a transcript is missing, say so instead of inferring content. When the user wants a follow-up meeting, propose times first and schedule only after the user confirms.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Recap",
   "text": "Summarize decisions and action items of my last project meeting.",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "Meetings"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Chat context",
   "text": "What did the team say in chat about the open risks?",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "TeamsMessages"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Schedule",
   "text": "Find a 30 minute slot next week for a follow-up with the same attendees.",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "MeetingActions"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "Meetings"
  },
  {
   "name": "TeamsMessages"
  },
  {
   "name": "MeetingActions"
  },
  {
   "name": "People",
   "include_related_content": false
  }
 ],
 "user_overrides": [
  {
   "path": "$.capabilities[?(@.name == 'TeamsMessages')]",
   "allowed_actions": [
    "remove"
   ]
  }
 ],
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "Summaries can contain mistakes. Check decisions and owners before you share them."
 }
}

=== How to use ===
1. Check that meeting recording and transcription are turned on in your tenant, otherwise recaps have little to work with.
2. Decide whether to scope TeamsMessages to specific channels with urls. Without urls the agent can search all channels, meetings and chats of the user.
3. Test the scheduling flow with a user who owns the calendar of the attendees and with one who does not; time suggestions follow the user's permissions.
4. Brief users that they can remove the Teams messages source when a recap should rely on the transcript only.
5. Review summaries of sensitive meetings before sharing them.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Using user_overrides with a path that targets something other than a capability by name.
- Adding more than 5 URLs to TeamsMessages or more than 5 items to Meetings.
- Assuming the agent can recap meetings without transcripts; missing source data produces weak output.
- Giving the agent EmailActions or other write capabilities just because they sound useful; add only what the scenario needs.
- Leaving the disclaimer out for an agent that summarizes people's words.

A finance policy desk with fixed answers and files that ship inside the package. editorial_answers (schema 1.7 and later) holds question and answer pairs that are matched by semantic similarity, with optional similarity_thresholds (min and max from 0 to 10), so the most important answers stay identical for every employee. Everything else is answered from EmbeddedKnowledge files. default_response_mode is Quick response because the questions are short factual lookups. Schema v1.9.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: unrecognized properties make the whole manifest invalid; strings are limited to 4,000 characters unless stated; name is limited to 100 characters, description to 1,000, instructions to 8,000 and the disclaimer text to 500. editorial_answers uses either url or answers, never both, with at most 300 answers. EmbeddedKnowledge allows at most 10 files of 1 MB or less each, of type doc, docx, ppt, pptx, xls, xlsx, txt or pdf; paths are relative to the manifest.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Contoso Finance Policy Desk",
 "description": "Answers questions about travel, expenses and procurement rules with fixed answers for the most common questions.",
 "instructions": "You answer questions about Contoso finance policies.\n\nUse the editorial answers when they match. Otherwise answer only from the embedded policy files and cite the file name and section. If a case is not covered, tell the employee to contact finance-help@contoso.example. Do not approve exceptions. Do not calculate tax. Keep answers short and use the employee's language.",
 "behavior_overrides": {
  "default_response_mode": "Quick response",
  "special_instructions": {
   "discourage_model_knowledge": true
  }
 },
 "editorial_answers": {
  "answers": [
   {
    "question": "What is the daily meal allowance for business trips?",
    "answer": "The daily meal allowance is set per country in section 4 of the Travel Policy. Check the table for your destination before you book.",
    "similarity_thresholds": {
     "min": 3,
     "max": 8
    }
   },
   {
    "question": "Until when do I have to submit expense reports?",
    "answer": "Submit expense reports within 30 days after the end of the trip or the purchase, with all receipts attached.",
    "similarity_thresholds": {
     "min": 3,
     "max": 8
    }
   }
  ]
 },
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "EmbeddedKnowledge",
   "files": [
    {
     "file": "policies/travel-policy.pdf"
    },
    {
     "file": "policies/expense-policy.docx"
    },
    {
     "file": "policies/procurement-rules.pdf"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Meals",
   "text": "What is the daily meal allowance for a trip to Vienna?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Deadline",
   "text": "How long do I have to submit my expense report?"
  },
  {
   "title": "Purchasing",
   "text": "Do I need three quotes for a purchase of 8000 euros?"
  }
 ],
 "disclaimer": {
  "text": "Policy summaries only. Exceptions need written approval from Finance."
 }
}

=== How to use ===
1. Create the policies folder in the app package and copy the three files into it; keep every file below 1 MB.
2. Write editorial answers only for facts that must never vary and review them whenever the policy changes. Start with similarity thresholds like the ones shown and tune them with real questions.
3. If you have more than a handful of answers, store them in a separate file and reference it with editorial_answers.url instead of inlining them.
4. Version the package when a policy changes, because embedded files are static copies; use SharePoint knowledge instead if the policy changes weekly.
5. Test with questions that are close to, but not the same as, an editorial question to see where the threshold works.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Using both url and answers in editorial_answers.
- Embedding files that are larger than 1 MB or of an unsupported type such as csv.
- Letting embedded files go stale. The agent will quote the version inside the package, not the current intranet page.
- Setting thresholds so low that unrelated questions receive a fixed answer.
- Embedding files that contain personal data or confidential content: anyone with access to the agent can retrieve their content.

A field service agent that uses the two newest parts of declarative agent schema v1.9 together with older ones: agent_skills (up to 8 folders, each containing a SKILL.md file that follows the Agent Skills standard), the Fabric capability that grounds answers in Microsoft Fabric data, CodeInterpreter for calculations and charts, an API plugin action for repair data, and a restricted WebSearch capability for manuals. In 1.9 the instructions property became optional; this manifest still sets it because the agent needs rules. Skill folder names and the plugin file are placeholders.

=== File: declarativeAgent.json ===
Comment: unrecognized properties make the whole manifest invalid; strings are limited to 4,000 characters unless stated; name is limited to 100 characters, description to 1,000, instructions to 8,000 and the disclaimer text to 500. Each agent_skills entry has one required property, folder: a relative path from the manifest to a directory that contains SKILL.md. Use forward slashes; the path must stay inside the app package and cannot use ../.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.9/schema.json",
 "version": "v1.9",
 "name": "Contoso Field Service Assistant",
 "description": "Supports field technicians with repair lookups, ticket analysis, maintenance data from Fabric and quick calculations.",
 "instructions": "You support field service technicians.\n\nUse the repair-lookup skill to find repair history of a device and the ticket-analysis skill to find patterns across tickets. Use Fabric data for maintenance intervals and failure rates, and use the code interpreter for calculations or charts. Always state the data source and the time range. Never suggest bypassing a safety procedure; refer to the official manual instead.",
 "conversation_starters": [
  {
   "title": "Repair history",
   "text": "Show the repair history of device pump-4711."
  },
  {
   "title": "Failure pattern",
   "text": "Which components failed most often in the last six months?",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "Fabric"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Chart",
   "text": "Chart the monthly ticket count by category for this year.",
   "depends_on": [
    {
     "name": "capabilities",
     "id": "CodeInterpreter"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "capabilities": [
  {
   "name": "Fabric"
  },
  {
   "name": "CodeInterpreter"
  },
  {
   "name": "WebSearch",
   "sites": [
    {
     "url": "https://docs.contoso.example/manuals"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "actions": [
  {
   "id": "repairsApi",
   "file": "ai-plugin.json"
  }
 ],
 "agent_skills": [
  {
   "folder": "./skills/repair-lookup"
  },
  {
   "folder": "./skills/ticket-analysis"
  }
 ]
}

=== Package layout (text) ===
Comment: this is the folder structure the manifest expects. Keep the skill files short and task-specific.
appPackage/
  manifest.json
  declarativeAgent.json
  ai-plugin.json
  repairs-openapi.json
  color.png
  outline.png
  skills/
    repair-lookup/SKILL.md
    ticket-analysis/SKILL.md

=== How to use ===
1. Write one SKILL.md per task: when to use the skill, the steps, and what to return. Keep each skill focused so that the agent can pick the right one.
2. Check that your users have the licenses and access that the Fabric capability needs, and that the Fabric data they should see is shared with them.
3. Keep the manual site in WebSearch to one trusted documentation host.
4. Reference the manifest from the app manifest and test each starter; the Fabric and CodeInterpreter starters appear only when those capabilities are present.
5. Move stable procedures out of instructions into skills so that the instruction text stays short.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Pointing a skill folder outside the package or using ../ in the path.
- Declaring more than 8 skills.
- Forgetting that a skill folder without SKILL.md is not a skill.
- Adding Fabric without checking who may access the underlying data; the agent shows what the user can read.
- Assuming schema 1.9 is available in every client; check that your target tenants support the version before you ship.

A custom engine agent is a bot that brings its own orchestration and models into Microsoft 365 Copilot. Two things must be defined together or the manifest fails validation: the copilot value in bots[].scopes and an entry in copilotAgents.customEngineAgents. The agent's id must equal the botId, and that bot must also have the personal scope. This sample adds command suggestions for the copilot scope (each command has a title, a description and a type, optionally a prompt), a disclaimer and webApplicationInfo for single sign-on. App manifest 1.30; GUIDs and domains are placeholders.

=== File: manifest.json ===
Comment: required root properties are manifestVersion, version, id, developer, name, description, icons and accentColor. Limits: developer.name 32 characters, name.short 30, name.full 100, description.short 80, description.full 4000, packageName 64; short and full values must differ. bots allows one bot per app; commandLists allows up to three entries and each entry needs scopes and commands. A prompt command needs a prompt string. In customEngineAgents, id and type are required and type must be bot.
{
 "$schema": "https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/teams/v1.30/MicrosoftTeams.schema.json",
 "manifestVersion": "1.30",
 "version": "1.0.0",
 "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
 "packageName": "example.contoso.supportagent",
 "developer": {
  "name": "Contoso Digital",
  "websiteUrl": "https://contoso.example",
  "privacyUrl": "https://contoso.example/privacy",
  "termsOfUseUrl": "https://contoso.example/terms"
 },
 "name": {
  "short": "Support Agent",
  "full": "Contoso Customer Support Agent"
 },
 "description": {
  "short": "A custom engine agent for support cases in Copilot and Teams.",
  "full": "The Contoso Customer Support Agent runs on Contoso's own orchestration and language models and is available in Microsoft 365 Copilot and in a personal chat in Teams. No external account is required; sign-in uses your Microsoft work account."
 },
 "icons": {
  "outline": "outline.png",
  "color": "color.png"
 },
 "accentColor": "#6264A7",
 "bots": [
  {
   "botId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
   "scopes": [
    "personal",
    "copilot"
   ],
   "supportsFiles": false,
   "isNotificationOnly": false,
   "commandLists": [
    {
     "scopes": [
      "personal",
      "copilot"
     ],
     "commands": [
      {
       "title": "Case status",
       "description": "Show the status of a support case.",
       "type": "prompt",
       "prompt": "Show the status of my open support cases."
      },
      {
       "title": "Escalate",
       "description": "Explain how to escalate a case.",
       "type": "basic"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "copilotAgents": {
  "customEngineAgents": [
   {
    "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
    "type": "bot",
    "disclaimer": {
     "text": "This agent uses AI and can make mistakes. Check important answers."
    }
   }
  ]
 },
 "webApplicationInfo": {
  "id": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111",
  "resource": "api://contoso.example/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111"
 },
 "validDomains": [
  "contoso.example"
 ]
}

=== How to use ===
1. Register the bot (Azure Bot resource or Developer Portal), host the endpoint and implement the conversation logic with the Microsoft 365 Agents SDK or your own stack.
2. Replace the placeholder botId, the agent id (same GUID) and the webApplicationInfo values, then set up SSO in Microsoft Entra ID as described for bot single sign-on.
3. Keep the personal scope in the list; the agent id must refer to a bot that has it.
4. Package, sideload and open the agent from the Copilot agent list; test the two commands and the disclaimer text.
5. Decide whether you want functionsAs and agentic user templates; the default is agentOnly, so the sample leaves them out.

=== Common mistakes ===
- Defining the copilot scope without copilotAgents.customEngineAgents, or the other way round. Both produce a validation error.
- Using different GUIDs for botId and the agent id.
- Leaving out personal from scopes. The referenced bot must have the personal scope.
- Putting more than one bot or more than three command lists into the manifest.
- Treating custom engine agents as stable GA features: the Microsoft pages call parts of this area preview, so check the current status.

A meeting app that appears in the meeting chat, the meeting details page, the side panel and on the meeting stage. A configurable tab with the group chat scope and the meeting contexts hosts the experience, meetingSurfaces lists where it can show (sidePanel and stage), and meetingExtensionDefinition sets meeting-wide options such as anonymous guest support. Resource-specific consent in the authorization block lets the app read participant information (delegated) and send meeting notifications (application) for the chats it is installed in; webApplicationInfo with the Entra app ID is needed for RSC. App manifest 1.30; GUIDs and URLs are placeholders.

=== File: manifest.json ===
Comment: required root properties are manifestVersion, version, id, developer, name, description, icons and accentColor. Limits: developer.name 32 characters, name.short 30, name.full 100, description.short 80, description.full 4000, packageName 64; short and full values must differ. configurableTabs: context may include meetingChatTab, meetingDetailsTab, meetingSidePanel and meetingStage; meetingSurfaces allows at most two values; supportedPlatform allows desktop, mobile and teamsMeetingDevices. meetingExtensionDefinition properties are all optional and default to false.
